    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b795dd80_82c6f087",
        "filename": "core/jni/com_android_internal_util_ArrayUtils.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 93,
      "author": {
        "id": 1137063
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-07T02:01:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Does GetPrimitiveArrayCritical() guarantee that the copy never happens, even if the array is movable?  (Assuming Android ART specifically, not an arbitrary Java implementation.)  If so, using it and asserting that a copy did not happen would make sense.  Otherwise, we would not want this to crash just because the caller passed in a movable array; they *should* use a non-movable one, but code does not always control the allocation site and we just need to do the best we can.",
      "parentUuid": "66dbdca8_df688fa8",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 93,
        "startChar": 53,
        "endLine": 93,
        "endChar": 73
      },
      "revId": "47fc4c7603ccb8cae401c6bb4fec1044365b8a8e",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2bd0d11d_7fbf8c0d",
        "filename": "core/jni/com_android_internal_util_ArrayUtils.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 93,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042828
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-09T00:15:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The ART implementation never copies. See https://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/main/+/main:art/runtime/jni/jni_internal.cc;l\u003d2385-2387\n\nPerformance in the movable case may not be great, since we may end up waiting for (parts of) the GC.",
      "parentUuid": "b795dd80_82c6f087",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 93,
        "startChar": 53,
        "endLine": 93,
        "endChar": 73
      },
